3. PERMITTED PURPOSES

Permitted Purposes are:

(a) academic study, classroom instruction, coursework, and scholarly
    research;
(b) independent noncommercial study, experimentation, and research; and
(c) good-faith investigation, comparison, verification, and replication of
    the Software's methods, correctness, performance, reliability, or
    security, and preparation of research findings about those subjects.

These permissions include research prototypes, test harnesses, benchmark
comparisons, and modifications needed to reproduce or challenge claims
about Mirmir or its use of the ASH Model. They are not limited to research
that supports the Licensor's claims.

Ordinary tuition, research grants, researcher salaries, and ordinary
scholarly publication fees do not, by themselves, make an otherwise
Permitted Purpose commercial. A person's employer or a project's funding
source alone does not determine eligibility. However, the actual activity
must fall within the purposes above and may not include the prohibited
activities in Section 4. An academic affiliation or research label does
not authorize commercial product development or operational deployment.

